{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 self-defeats by labeling the lease-mismatch guard as Priority 2 rather than first, while simultaneously asserting that skipping the test causes a 'security-policy bypass' where 'requests with invalid leases are accepted.' A policy bypass permitting unauthorized routing is a primary corruption vector, yet SRC_001 deliberately demotes it below a higher-priority test it does not detail here. The problem demands an explicit ordering justified by blast radius; SRC_001 inverts its own justification by placing the most severe, acceptance-side bypass second, contradicting its own blast-radius reasoning.","target":"026a1c585082fa8d592d0acb829a23b925344c26d8ebd0f380b1b8ff045ba507"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 conflates a mutation-test meta-check with the required single cheapest regression test. The problem asks for 'the single cheapest test that would catch the guard dying,' but SRC_002 proposes monkeypatching SCHOOL_ROUTE_LEASE_MISMATCH to pass-through and then asserting the suite catches it — that is a test-of-the-test-suite audit, not a direct regression test. A maintainer cannot 'implement test 1 tomorrow morning' from this because it requires modifying production code and rerunning the whole suite, which is not a minimal regression test.","target":"4b06c65bdeaaf6e5587911c0a57d0cdf42262e9f21ca34fa7e9f2a060ff70d30"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 provides no explicit ordering relative to the other gaps beyond labeling itself 'Test #4' and vaguely saying it is fourth because the blast radius 'is a recovery path failure.' The problem requires an ordering justified by blast radius contrasting 'what corrupts the append-only record vs what merely hides a refusal'; SRC_003 gives no comparison to any record-corruption gap or any refusal-hiding gap, so its fourth-place position is asserted, not justified.","target":"7b7fbcf692a12e9df58f924c18267d9995fdb9f1d8393a649fc182cd1c90f0c7"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004 treats the no-scratch-exposure recovery guard's blast radius as merely leaving the harness 'in an inconsistent state,' but it never anchors this in the problem's required blast-radius axis. The problem explicitly demands a justification contrasting record corruption vs refusal-hiding; a recovery-path failure that leaves the harness inconsistent is neither a refusal-hiding gap nor clearly a non-record-corruption gap. Without stating whether this corrupts the append-only record or not, SRC_004's priority-4 placement is unsupported relative to the required covering principle.","target":"b72a2849389a08a45e86b4834b960db4ebb598cd9c47f204c2c7ea80451ba009"}]}